<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Coahuila</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Coahuila is a state of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, with its capital at Saltillo. Under Spanish rule it was a frontier province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> exposed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanche</a> raids, and from 1824 it was joined with Texas as the Mexican state of Coahuila y Tejas until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas-revolution">Texas Revolution</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venustiano-carranza">Venustiano Carranza</a>, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-revolution">Mexican Revolution</a>, came from Coahuila.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/coahuila.jpg" alt="Coahuila" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BlatZzz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Espiga_de_trigo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Coahuila borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuevo-leon">Nuevo León</a> to the east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a> to the south and Durango and Chihuahua to the west. To the north it holds a 512 km stretch of the border with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>. With an area of 151,563 km2 it is the third-largest state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. The Sierra Madre Oriental crosses it from north-west to south-east, and west of the range the land lies in the Chihuahuan Desert. The state is largely arid or semi-arid, but its rivers support irrigated farming, especially of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, and the Parras district produces wine and brandy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Spanish explored the north some decades after their victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>, and the harsher climate and the absence of gold delayed them. The first Spanish settlement in the region was at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Minas</a> de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> in 1577, and Saltillo was settled in 1586 as part of the province of Nueva Vizcaya in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>. Tlaxcalan settlers founded a community beside Saltillo, San Esteban de Nueva Tlaxcala.</p><p>Under the Constitution of 1824 Coahuila y Tejas became a state of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, first with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuevo-leon">Nuevo León</a>, which was detached later that year, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, which stayed until it seceded in 1836. Monclova was the capital from 1833 to 1835. In 1840 Coahuila was briefly part of the Republic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a>. In the mid-19th century the Sánchez Navarro family owned a ranch of 6,700,000 hectares, mostly in Coahuila, the largest privately owned property in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanche</a> raiders attacked the state often in the 1840s and 1850s. On 19 February 1856 Santiago Vidaurri annexed Coahuila to Nuevo León, and it regained separate status in 1868. In the Mexican Revolution Francisco Villa attacked Torreón.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Coahuila de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, named for General Ignacio Zaragoza, who was born there, is one of the 31 states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. Its capital is Saltillo, and its other main cities are Torreón, Monclova, Piedras Negras and Ciudad Acuña. The state has two biosphere reserves, Maderas del Carmen and the Cuatro Ciénegas Basin. Venustiano Carranza and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-i-madero">Francisco I. Madero</a>, both presidents of Mexico, came from Coahuila.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Coahuila” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Mexican independence in 1821 Stephen F. Austin led the Old Three Hundred into the province and other empresarios followed; in 1824 Texas was joined with <b>Coahuila</b> as the state of Coahuila y Tejas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-texas">Republic of Texas · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In Mexico Apache communities live in Sonora, Chihuahua, <b>Coahuila</b> and Tamaulipas.</blockquote><a href="/culture/apache">Apache · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The commandant general held military, political and judicial authority over Nueva Vizcaya, Sonora and Sinaloa, the Californias, New Mexico, <b>Coahuila</b> and Texas, and at times Nuevo León and Nuevo Santander.</blockquote><a href="/state/commandancy-general-of-the-internal-provinces">Commandancy General of the Internal Provinces</a></li><li><blockquote>Opposition to the Centralist Republic of Mexico ran deep in the northern frontier states of Tamaulipas, Nuevo León and <b>Coahuila</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-the-rio-grande">Republic of the Rio Grande</a></li></ul></section>
